1a.For the purposes of Articles 9 and 10, the Commission shall adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 by [OP: Please insert the date = six months after the date of entry into force of this Regulation] to supplement this Regulation by establishing, for products and services covered by Annex II and Annex III, the third countries or specific manufacturers from which content shall be treated as equivalent to content of Union origin, provided that all of the following criteria are met:
(a) that third country provides national treatment related to Union products or entities under an agreement establishing a free trade area or a customs union, and effective reciprocity and comparable market access for Union products or economic operators are ensured in law and in practice. This shall include cases where laws, regulations or practices do not reserve public contracts for domestic products or suppliers, do not grant them a preference, do not impose local-content requirements and do not otherwise materially restrict access for Union products or economic operators;
(b) inclusion of that third country does not create dependencies or other developments that may threaten the security of supply in the Union of the products in question;
(c) reliable enforcement of applicable rules of origin is ensured and there is no material risk of circumvention of the Union origin requirements, including through re-export, trans-shipment or minimal processing; and
(d) inclusion of that third country is not precluded by any other exception under the applicable agreement.

1a.Content of the following origin shall be equivalent to Union origin for the purposes of Articles 11, 12, 13, and 14:
(a) content originating in a partner country;
(b) content produced by a Strategic Project recognised under Article 6 of Regulation (EU) 2024/1252 (Critical Raw Materials Act), for the duration of that recognition.

1b.A third country shall be deemed a partner country for all sectors listed in Annex I from the date of entry into force of this Regulation, where it:
(a) meets all of the following criteria:
(i) it is a party to the Agreement on Government Procurement;
(ii) it has concluded or signed with the Union a customs union or an agreement establishing a free trade area, where relevant obligations of the Union exist under that agreement;
(iii) it is a full member of the International Energy Agency; and
(iv) it is a party to the Paris Agreement.

1e.The Commission shall adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 to include, in whole or in part, a third country as a partner country for all sectors listed in Annex I.
A third country shall be eligible for inclusion only where it is a party to the Agreement on Government Procurement, or where it has concluded with the Union a customs union or an agreement establishing a free trade area, where relevant obligations of the Union exist under that agreement.
In deciding whether to include an eligible third country, the Commission shall assess the following four selection criteria:
(a) whether that third country provides, de jure and de facto, effective reciprocal treatment and market access to Union products or entities across the relevant levels of government, in the sector concerned;
(b) whether inclusion contributes to the diversification of Union supply chains in the sector concerned and the resilience of the Single Market and thereby contributes to the Union's economic security and energy security, in particular where Union sourcing is concentrated in a single third country. In assessing this, the Commission shall have regard to the sectoral cooperation the Union is pursuing with that country, such as a Clean Trade and Investment Partnership, a Green Alliance or a Critical Raw Materials Strategic Partnership;
(c) whether inclusion of that third country creates a risk of circumvention of the Union origin requirements set out in this Regulation, in particular through the re-export of products or components originating in a third country holding more than 40% of global manufacturing capacity in the sector concerned, as well as through the establishment on its territory of undertakings, whose main purpose is to obtain access to the internal market;
(d) whether that third country implements the Paris Agreement, such as through domestic carbon pricing mechanisms or measures having equivalent effect.

2a.With respect to the Union-origin requirements referred to in Article 11, the Commission may adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 in order to establish, in whole or in part, content originating in a third country with which the Union has concluded an agreement establishing a free trade area or a customs union, or are member of the WTO Government Procurement Agreement to be of Union origin if all the following conditions are fulfilled:
(a) the third country has provided, in law and in practice, national treatment related to Union products or entities in relation to all contracting authorities or entities, covering all sectors listed in Annex -Ia, in the framework of the established agreement;
(b) the third country has fulfilled its obligation of full and effective implementation of its agreement towards all Member States without reserve and in a non-discriminatory manner;
(c) such inclusion is consistent with the objective to avoid dependencies and with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security of supply chains for the products in question, energy security, and the resilience of the Single Market. The third country contributes to resilience and security of Union supply chains and economic security objectives of the Union in the sectors listed in Annex -Ia. This should be assessed using the following elements:
i) the level of reduction of trade dependencies via diversification of suppliers;
ii) the level of integration in Union supply chains;
iii) the level of investments made or planned in the Union;
iv) the level of alignment with Union investment, export control and sanction policies
v) the risk of circumvention as referred to in article 9a
c) the third country has implemented or is preparing to implement a carbon pricing scheme comparable to the Union ETS;
d) the third country upholds equivalent environmental, social and human rights standards. This should be assessed using the following criteria:
i) the third country is a signatory to the Paris Agreement and is upholding its commitments under that Agreement.; and
ii) the third country has ratified and implemented all fundamental ILO conventions and is committed to the ILO Declaration on Social Justice for a Fair Globalization, in particular to decent working conditions for all, wages, working hours, social protection, labour standards, skills training and gender equality. and provides effective labour protection including as regards freedom of association, collective bargaining, occupational safety and health, information and consultation of workers, equal treatment and non-discrimination
iii) Protects and promotes human rights by implementing the United Nations Global Compact and the United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights Equivalence is presumed when a third country has concluded and implemented a sanctions-based trade and sustainable development chapter with the EU.
The Commission shall monitor compliance with the conditions laid down in this paragraph. Where the Commission finds, on its own initiative or following a reasoned request from the European Parliament or the Council, that those conditions are no longer met, it shall, within three months of that finding, adopt a delegated act in accordance with Article 30 to exclude a third country from any delegated act adopted pursuant to this paragraph. This delegated act should allow for a transition period of no longer than 12 months after its adoption.

2a.With respect to the Union-origin requirements referred to in Article 11, the Commission may adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 in order to establish, in whole or in part, content originating in a third country with which the Union has concluded an agreement establishing a free trade area or a customs union, or are member of the WTO Government Procurement Agreement to be of Union origin if all the following conditions are fulfilled:
(a) the third country has provided, in law and in practice, national treatment related to Union products or entities in relation to all contracting authorities or entities, covering all sectors listed in Annex -Ia;
(b) such inclusion is consistent with the objective to avoid dependencies and with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security of supply chains for the products in question, energy security, and the resilience of the Single Market. The third country contributes to resilience and security of Union supply chains and economic security objectives of the Union in the sectors listed in Annex -Ia. This should be assessed using the following elements:
i) the level of reduction of trade dependencies via diversification of suppliers ;
ii) the level of integration in Union supply chains ;
iii) the level of investments made or planned in the Union;
iv) the level of alignment with Union investment, export control and sanction policies
v) the risk of circumvention as referred to in article 9a
c) the third country has implemented or is preparing to implement a carbon pricing scheme comparable to the Union ETS;
d) the third country upholds equivalent environmental, social and human rights standards. This should be assessed using the following criteria:
i) the third country is a signatory to the Paris Agreement and is upholding its commitments under that Agreement.; and
ii) the third country has ratified and implemented all fundamental ILO conventions and is committed to the ILO Declaration on Social Justice for a Fair Globalization, in particular to decent working conditions for all, wages, working hours, social protection, labour standards, skills training and gender equality. and provides effective labour protection including as regards freedom of association, collective bargaining, occupational safety and health, information and consultation of workers, equal treatment and non-discrimination
iii) Protects and promotes human rights by implementing the United Nations Global Compact and the United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights
Equivalence is presumed when a third country has concluded and implemented a sanctions-based trade and sustainable development chapter with the EU.
The Commission shall monitor compliance with the conditions laid down in this paragraph. Where the Commission finds, on its own initiative or following a reasoned request from the European Parliament or the Council, that those conditions are no longer met, it shall, within three months of that finding, adopt a delegated act in accordance with Article 30 to exclude a third country from any delegated act adopted pursuant to this paragraph. This delegated act should allow for a transition period of no longer than 12 months after its adoption.

2a.With respect to the Union-origin requirements referred to in Articles 11, 12 and 13, the Commission shall adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 to recognise, in whole or in part, content originating in a third country as being of Union origin if all the following conditions are fulfilled:
(a) the third country has provided, in law and in practice, national treatment related to Union products or entities under agreements establishing a free trade area or a customs union or the Agreement on Government Procurement, in relation to all the sectors listed in Annex I;
(b) such inclusion is consistent with the objective to avoid dependencies and with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security of supply chains for the products in question, energy security, and the resilience of the Single Market;
(c) the recognition does not create a significant risk of distortions of competition in the Union market arising from subsidies or other forms of state support granted by the third country in respect of the products concerned;
(d) the third country complies with the commitment to implement the Paris Agreement through its nationally determined contributions;
(e) where the third country has established, or is planning to establish, demand-side measures for low-carbon industrial products or public support schemes comparable to those referred to in Article 12, those measures or schemes provide, in law and in practice, national treatment to Union products or entities;
(f) the inclusion of the third country does not entail a risk of circumvention of the Union origin requirements set out in this Regulation, in particular through the re-export of products or components originating in third countries which are not contained in a delegated act adopted based on this Article, or through the establishment on its territory of undertakings owned and controlled by entities established in third countries that are not within the scope of this Article with the purpose to access to the Union market for such products or components.
Where the above conditions are no longer met, the Commission shall adopt a delegated act in accordance with Article 30 to exclude in whole or in part a third country from any delegated act adopted on this paragraph.
For third countries meeting all of the following criteria, the Commission shall adopt the delegated acts referred to in the first subparagraph no later than three months after the date of entry into force of this Regulation, provided that the conditions set out in points (a) to (f) are fulfilled:
(i) it is a party to the Agreement on Government Procurement;
(ii) it has concluded or signed with the Union an agreement establishing a free trade area or a customs union, where relevant obligations of the Union exist under that agreement;
(iii) it is a full member of the International Energy Agency;
and (iv) it is a party to the Paris Agreement.

2a.With a view to ensuring that the requirements laid down in this Regulation are commensurate with the industrial capacity available in the Union, an ex-ante verification mechanism shall be established to assess the availability of Union industrial capacity before any new mandatory requirement concerning Union origin or low-carbon content becomes applicable. That mechanism shall also regularly monitor the development of Union industrial capacity, with a view to determining whether any adjustments to the applicable requirements are necessary and, where appropriate, enabling such adjustments to be introduced. For each product, material or component concerned, the mechanism shall assess at least:
(a) the current production capacity available within the Union;
(b) the number of suppliers established and operating within the Union;
(c) the price differential compared with equivalent alternatives available on international markets;
(d) the quality of the product and its compliance with applicable technical and certification requirements;
(e) the impact on SMEs, downstream undertakings and exporting undertakings; and
(f) the risk of creating or reinforcing strategic dependencies, whether within or outside the Union.
The information gathered via this mechanism shall be used for the subsequent specification of different minimum percentages of Union content, taking into account both the geographical scope used to determine Union origin and the products, materials or components to which those percentages apply.

2a.By way of derogation from paragraph 3, until [OP: please insert the date = 6 years after the date of entry into force of this Regulation], steel which has obtained Union origin in accordance with paragraph 2 shall be considered to be of Union origin where the last substantial transformation conferring that origin took place in an installation which fulfils all of the following conditions:
(a) the installation is located in the Union and is operated by an undertaking established in the Union;
(b) on 4 March 2026, the installation was carrying out activities classified under NACE Rev. 2 group 24.1, 24.2 or 24.3. The first subparagraph shall apply without prejudice to restrictive measures adopted under Article 215 TFEU.

2a.Project-tailored holistic “Made in EU” criteria should encompass:
(a) The ownership and control of Intellectual Property (IP) rights associated with components and sub-components;
(b) The European location of manufacturing and processing operations for sub-components;
(c) The local workforce used for the manufacturing/assembly of the final component/subcomponent;
(d) Gross Value Added in the EU;
(e) The location of long-term maintenance and service contracts in the EU.
When implementing major infrastructure projects related to certain technologies (as defined in Annex II to Regulation (EU) 2024/1735), Member States shall monitor these performance indicators.

2b.With respect to the Union-origin requirements referred to in Article 11, the Commission may adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 in order to establish, in whole or in part, content originating in a third country to be of Union origin if all the following conditions are fulfilled:
(a) the third country has provided, in law and in practice, national treatment related to Union products or entities under agreements establishing a free trade area or a customs union or the Agreement on Government Procurement, in relation to all the sectors listed in Annexes II and III regarding public procurement and public support schemes;
(b) such inclusion is consistent with the objective to avoid dependencies and with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security of supply chains for the products in question, energy security, and the resilience of the Single Market;
(c) the third country has a legal framework for competition that is substantially equivalent to that applying in the Union single market, including in respect to environmental due diligence and greenhouse gas emission reporting, and has ratified and effectively implements the ILO Fundamental Conventions;
(d) the third country complies with the commitment to implement the Paris Agreement through its national determined contributions and implements domestic carbon prices mechanisms or measures having equivalent effects;
(e) the third country protects and promotes human rights by implementing the United Nations Global Compact and the United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, abide by rules and standards equivalent to OECD Due diligence guidance for responsible business conduct, in particular the guidelines related to combatting corruption, the OECD due diligence guidance for responsible supply chains of minerals from conflict-affected and high-risk areas, and the OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Meaningful Stakeholder Engagement in the Extractive Sector, including where referring to the principles set out in the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples ;
(f)the third country has established, or is committed to establishing within a defined timeframe, demand-side measures for low-carbon industrial products comparable to those provided for in this Regulation and which provide national treatment to Union products or entities;
(g) the inclusion of the third country does not entail a risk of circumvention of the Union origin requirements set out in this Regulation, in particular through the re-export of products or components originating in third countries which are not contained in a delegated act adopted based on this Article, or through the establishment on its territory of undertakings owned and controlled by entities established in third countries which are not contained in a delegated act adopted based on this Article with the purpose to access to the Union market for such products or components.
The Commission shall regularly monitor with a view to assessing whether the third countries still meet the conditions listed under this Regulation. This monitoring exercise shall take place at least once a year.
Where the above conditions are no longer met, the Commission shall adopt a delegated act in accordance with Article 30 to exclude in whole or in part a third country from any delegated act adopted on this paragraph.

2b.With respect to the Union-origin requirements referred to in Article 11, the Commission may adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 in order to establish, in whole or in part, content originating in a third country to be of Union origin if all the following conditions are fulfilled:
(a) the third country has provided, in law and in practice, national treatment related to Union products or entities under agreements establishing a free trade area or a customs union or the Agreement on Government Procurement, in relation to all the sectors listed in Annex I;
(b) such inclusion is consistent with the objective to avoid dependencies and with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security of supply chains for the products in question, energy security, and the resilience of the Single Market;
(c) the third country has a legal framework for competition that is substantially equivalent to that applying in the Union single market, including in respect to environmental due diligence and greenhouse gas emission reporting, and has ratified and effectively implements the ILO Fundamental Conventions;
(d) the third country complies with the commitment to implement the Paris Agreement through its national determined contributions and implements domestic carbon prices mechanisms or measures having equivalent effects;
(e) the third country protects and promotes human rights by implementing the United Nations Global Compact and the United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights;
(f) the third country has established, or is committed to establishing within a defined timeframe, demand-side measures for low-carbon industrial products comparable to those provided for in this Regulation and which provide national treatment to Union products or entities;
(g) the inclusion of the third country does not entail a risk of circumvention of the Union origin requirements set out in this Regulation, in particular through the re-export of products or components originating in third countries which are not contained in a delegated act adopted based on this Article, or through the establishment on its territory of undertakings owned and controlled by entities established in third countries that are not within the scope of this Article with the purpose to access to the Union market for such products or components.
Where the above conditions are no longer met, the Commission shall adopt a delegated act in accordance with Article 30 to exclude in whole or in part a third country from any delegated act adopted on this paragraph.

2b.With respect to the Union-origin requirements referred to in Article 11, the Commission may adopt delegated acts in accordance with Article 30 in order to establish, in whole or in part, content originating in a third country to be of Union origin if all the following conditions are fulfilled:
(a) the third country has provided, in law and in practice, national treatment related to Union products or entities under agreements establishing a free trade area or a customs union or the Agreement on Government Procurement, in relation to all the sectors listed in Annex I;
(b) such inclusion is consistent with the objective to avoid dependencies and with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security of supply chains for the products in question, energy security, and the resilience of the Single Market;
(c) the third country has a legal framework for competition that is substantially equivalent to that applying in the Union single market, including in respect to environmental due diligence and greenhouse gas emission reporting, and has ratified and effectively implements the ILO Fundamental Conventions;
(d) the third country complies with the commitment to implement the Paris Agreement through its national determined contributions and implements domestic carbon prices mechanisms or measures having equivalent effects;
(e) the third country protects and promotes human rights by implementing the United Nations Global Compact and the United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights;
(f) the third country has established, or is committed to establishing within a defined timeframe, demand-side measures for low-carbon industrial products comparable to those provided for in this Regulation and which provide national treatment to Union products or entities;
(g) the inclusion of the third country does not entail a risk of circumvention of the Union origin requirements set out in this Regulation, in particular through the re-export of products or components originating in third countries which are not contained in a delegated act adopted based on this Article, or through the establishment on its territory of undertakings owned and controlled by entities established in third countries that are not within the scope of this Article with the purpose to access to the Union market for such products or components.
Where the above conditions are no longer met, the Commission shall adopt a delegated act in accordance with Article 30 to exclude in whole or in part a third country from any delegated act adopted on this paragraph.

Article 7a
Sectoral action plans
1. The Commission shall establish the sectoral action plans referred to in this Regulation.
Those plans shall:
a) set out, for each relevant industrial sector and value chain, a coherent strategy and clear objectives — including, for example, the recovery of market share, job creation, and the reduction of strategic dependencies — and implementation pathways aimed at strengthening the Union’s industrial capacity, resilience and decarbonisation in line with the Union’s climate and environmental objectives;
b) identify the main challenges and bottlenecks affecting the development of the sector, including those related to research and innovation, investment including in production capacity and related to ports infrastructures, supply chains, skills, regulatory barriers, trade dynamics and propose appropriate solutions to address them;
c) ensure the coordinated mobilisation of relevant Union instruments, in particular those under this legislation, the Clean Industrial Deal, state aids rules, trade defence instruments;
d) adapt the tools established under this Regulation to the specific needs of each sector and value chain, including through the use of existing delegated powers, where appropriate.
2. The preparation and implementation of the sectoral action plans shall not delay the application of the general provisions of this Regulation.

1.1. With respect to the Union origin requirements referred to in Article 11, content originating in third countries shall be deemed to be of Union origin where:
a) content originates from members of the European Free Trade Associations (EFTA) which are members of the European Economic Area (EEA), and the United Kingdom, or
b) all of the following conditions are met in respect of the third country concerned:
(i) the third country accords national treatment to Union products and economic operators pursuant to an agreement establishing a free trade area or a customs union with the Union, or pursuant to the Agreement on Government Procurement, in respect of all sectors listed in Annex II and III;
(ii) the inclusion is consistent with the objective of strengthening the economic security of the Union, including the security and resilience of supply chains for the products concerned, energy security and the resilience of the internal market, and does not create or reinforce unhealthy strategic dependencies on third countries;
(iii) the third country respects and enforces internationally recognised labour rights and applicable labour law, including workers’ rights, freedom of association and collective bargaining;
(iv) the third country has in place, and effectively implements, a legal and regulatory framework that is substantially equivalent to that applicable within the Union internal market, including as regards environmental and climate protection standards, and has adopted and effectively implemented industrial sustainability measures comparable in their objectives and level of ambition to those applicable in the Union, including, where appropriate, a carbon pricing mechanism or equivalent measures;
(v) the inclusion of the third country does not give rise to a risk of circumvention of the Union-origin requirements laid down in this Regulation, in particular through the re-export of products or components originating in third countries that are not covered by a delegated act adopted pursuant to this Article, or through the establishment, on the territory of the third country concerned, of undertakings owned or controlled by entities established in third countries not covered by this Article, where such establishment is intended primarily to obtain access to the Union market for those products or components.
(vi) in respect of products originating in that third country falling within the sectors covered by Annexes II and III, reliable, verifiable and sufficiently detailed information is available to enable the competent authorities to assess compliance with the low-carbon requirements or with requirements relating to the use of sustainable carbon sources laid down pursuant to this Regulation.

