1. For the purposes of applying the Union origin requirements referred to in Article 25a, ‘originating content’ of a third country means content originating in:
(a) a third country that is a party to the GPA, provided that the procurement of the product in question falls within the scope of the procurement covered by the Union's Appendix I to the GPA, taking into account the applicable annexes;
(b) a third country with which the Union has concluded an agreement establishing a free-trade area or a customs union, under the conditions laid down in that agreement, provided that, under the agreement, relevant Union obligations exist for the product in question. The origin of products and components shall be determined in accordance with Article 60 of Regulation (EU) No 952/2013 of the European Parliament and of the Council.
2. The Commission shall adopt implementing acts pursuant to Article 5 of Regulation (EU) No 182/2011 to establish and update the lists of third countries providing partner originating content. In preparing those implementing acts, the Commission shall assess the implementation of the agreements referred to in paragraph 1, on the basis of the following criteria:
(a) the addition or removal of a third country is justified where that third country has granted, or has not granted, national treatment to Union products or entities under the agreements referred to in paragraph 1 in relation to any of the sectors listed in Annex I;
(b) the addition or removal of a third country is justified in order to avoid high-risk dependencies or any other development that could threaten the security of supply in the Union of the products concerned;
(c) the exclusion of a third country is justified on the basis of any other exception provided for under the applicable agreement;
(d) the actual degree of openness of the third country's public procurement markets to economic operators established in the EU and to goods and services originating in the Union, including at sub-federal, regional and local level;
(e) the existence of reservations, exclusions, sectoral limitations, thresholds, procedural conditions, localisation requirements, local content obligations, national preferences or other measures that directly or indirectly limit the access of Union economic operators, goods or services to the third country's public procurement;
(f) the comparability of the third country's regulatory framework with that of the Union as regards public procurement, transparency, competition, and environmental and social sustainability;
(g) the existence of fair competition conditions, including the absence of distortive subsidies, preferential treatment, selective advantages, discriminatory measures or practices capable of altering the level playing field between Union and third-country economic operators;
(h) the risk that the equivalence may allow or facilitate circumvention of the requirements referred to in Article 11;
(i) the risk that the equivalence may contribute to creating, consolidating or aggravating the Union's strategic dependencies in relation to products, components, technologies, raw materials, production capacities or value chains essential to the European industrial base. The implementing acts shall comply with the dispute-settlement procedures laid down in the relevant agreements concluded between the Union and a third country establishing a free-trade area or a customs union.
3. The Commission shall subsequently amend the country lists, where necessary, by means of further implementing acts pursuant to Article 5 of Regulation (EU) No 182/2011, where a third country becomes a party to an existing agreement or concludes a new agreement falling within the categories of agreements listed in paragraph 1, and/or where an amendment is justified by an assessment of the criteria referred to in paragraph 2.
4. Those implementing acts shall lay down the procedure for the periodic monitoring of the criteria referred to in paragraphs 2 and 3. The first implementing act establishing the lists of countries providing partner originating content shall be adopted by [OP: please insert the date = 6 months after the entry into force of this Regulation].
5. In order to prevent trade flows from being diverted towards third countries with which the Union has concluded an agreement establishing a free-trade area or a customs union, or which are parties to the Agreement on Government Procurement, the Commission shall continuously monitor and assess, at least annually, trade flows and market developments in order to identify: – a significant increase in imports suggestive of circumvention; – distortions identified in the course of trade-defence or anti-subsidy investigations; – discrepancies between trade flows and production capacity indicating transhipment. Where such circumstances are identified, the Commission shall adopt appropriate measures, including the suspension or limitation of the equivalence referred to in paragraph 1, and is empowered to adopt delegated acts pursuant to Article 30 to exclude, in whole or in part, a third country from the scope of paragraph 1.

