(da)Electricity grid technologies: For auctions published from [OP: please insert the date corresponding to one year after the entry into force of this Regulation]:
- for electricity grid technologies, the final products onshore substations and offshore substations with a voltage above 1 kV shall originate in the Union;
- for electric charging technologies for transport, the final products shall originate in the Union.

For the purposes of the first paragraph, the European industrial content of the project shall be assessed on the basis of the following criteria:
(a) the number and significance of main specific components and sub-components originating in the Union;
(b) the ownership and control by undertakings established in the Union of intellectual property rights and relevant know-how associated with main specific components and sub-components;
(c) the origin in the Union of materials used in the manufacture of main specific components and sub-components;
(d) the extent to which manufacturing, processing and assembly operations relating to main specific components and sub-components are carried out in the Union;
(e) the proportion of gross value added generated in the Union;
(f) the extent to which the workforce used for the manufacturing and assembly of main specific components and sub-components is located in the Union; and
(g) the extent to which long-term maintenance and servicing activities are carried out in the Union.
Where the application of the criteria referred to in points (a) and (c) requires the determination of the origin of goods, such origin shall be determined in accordance with the applicable Union rules on non-preferential origin. Member States shall determine the minimum levels to be achieved in relation to the criteria referred to in points (a) to (g), and may adapt those levels to the specific characteristics of the project concerned, taking into account, in particular, the technology and scale of the project, nuclear safety requirements, the availability of relevant technologies, components and materials in the Union, security and resilience of supply, the need to ensure effective competition and the need to avoid disproportionate increases in costs or delays in project implementation.
The Commission may adopt implementing acts establishing a common methodology for the assessment of the criteria referred to in points (a) to (g)

